Dear Judith,

Here is the table with my comments at the heading and the note. Bernard's t=
wo instalments are two receipts; Roux's single handover for three subscript=
ions is one. So 184 is the total number of times payments were entered as r=
eceived. It is not even a count of the people who handed over the money, le=
t alone a count of participants.

As we agreed this morning, I would use =E2=80=9CPayment entries=E2=80=9D. C=
ould you also put a sentence in the text saying that the denominator for re=
ceipts is different from the one needed for attendance? The accounts do not=
 tell us how many people came into the room.

Best,
Julie

On 12 January 2026 at 15:28, Judith Morris wrote:
> Dear Julie,
>
> Could we sit down at 10 on Wednesday 14 January with the working Table 2.=
1 and the box 12 receipt copies? I have attached the small draft so that yo=
u can open it at the old heading, =E2=80=9CParticipants=E2=80=9D. I am unea=
sy about what we have counted under it.
>
> On the paper copies on the desk, could we read the instalment at 043r lin=
e 4 alongside the later balance at 044r line 6, then look at the handover a=
t 049r line 5? I would like to check what each entry records before we deci=
de what the column can reasonably be called.
